# CANONICAL §7.2/§7.3/§9 fixture — a sketch glued to a solid FACE FOLLOWS that
# face, halts by name when it cannot, and a Cut that removes nothing is REFUSED
# (UX review 2026-09-14 WP-1 / B1 + N9, 2026-09-15).
#
# The design is `docs/design/astra/sketch-host-face-transport.md` (A1). What this
# file pins is the WIRE CONTRACT a Rust or frontend consumer branches on; the
# NUMBERS (frame transport vectors G1/G2/G4/G8/G9, the τGS conditioning boundary,
# the significance thresholds, the seat witness, the Cut εV) live in
# `worker/tests/test_sketch_host_reseat.cpp`. Same contract/geometry split as the
# component fixtures.
#
# Four legs, one document:
#
#   1. FOLLOW. The plate is re-planned from 40 mm to 25 mm thick. Its top cap moves
#      along its OWN normal and the sketch glued to it follows exactly: the step
#      carries `sketchPlacement` with the complete re-seated basis (origin z = 25,
#      the authored xAxis/yAxis/normal carried VERBATIM because n₁ == n₀), the
#      reported `translationMm` 15 / `rotationDeg` 0, and the worker-emitted
#      `SKETCH_HOST_RESEATED` info diagnostic. Every number here is a
#      PLANE-TO-PLANE distance between faces the extrude placed at exactly z=0 and
#      z=40 / z=25 — not a measurement — which is why it may be pinned at all (the
#      rule `place_component_mate_reseat.ndjson` follows). Before this build the
#      worker stored the sketch's raw params verbatim and the sketch stayed 15 mm
#      in the air with no diagnostic, silently cutting at the wrong plane.
#
#   2. HOST VANISHED. The same host sketch in a FROM-ZERO plan (§7.2 D5: an
#      `expectedBaseHash` equal to the empty-prefix anchor clones a genuinely empty
#      base) that no longer contains the extrude producing its body. The host
#      cannot resolve, so the Sketch step reports
#      `needsRepair` with the §9 item at `refId "op2.input0"` (the
#      `element_refs_mut` slot, NOT a wire `inputs[]` entry — `hostFace` is never
#      one, the `mate.target` rule) and the plan HALTS at m−1. The sketch is
#      Rust-owned document state and is never dropped; what stops is every
#      downstream feature that would otherwise cut at a guessed plane.
#
#   3. NON-PLANAR HOST. The same sketch pointed at the CYLINDER's lateral face:
#      planarity is a surface-TYPE predicate with no distance epsilon, so the step
#      halts with the op-built reason `sketchHostNonPlanar` rather than fitting an
#      approximate plane.
#
#   4. CUT THAT REMOVES NOTHING. A prism whose base plane is EXACTLY the plate's
#      top cap (z = 40) and which grows away from the solid: the boolean touches
#      the target and subtracts zero. `OP_FAILED` carrying `CUT_NO_EFFECT` at
#      `stage:"publish"`, riding `perStepResults[].diagnostics` because a failed
#      step emits no `planStep`. `volumeBefore`/`volumeAfter` are 240000 exactly
#      (100·60·40 of a box the extrude placed there); `toleranceMm3`,
#      `relErrBefore` and `relErrAfter` are MEASURED and therefore matched with
#      `$present`, never pinned.
#
# EVIDENCE. Every `hostFace` here carries the frozen `intent.descriptor` a real
# record is stamped with at mint (`document_runtime::stamp_intents`, through the new
# `element_refs_mut` `Sketch` arm). It is load-bearing for LEG 1: a from-zero plan
# clones an empty partition, so the tracked rung is unavailable by construction and
# the descriptor is the only thing that can re-find the cap.
#
# Leg 2 halts because the host's BODY is absent. EVERY unresolvable host halts the
# same way (§7.3 `hostFace`): a confidence shortfall raises the ladder's own §9 item
# with its own token and its ranked candidates, the step is `needsRepair`, and the
# plan stops at m−1. That is only honest because the frozen `anchor.worldPoint` is a
# point the kernel classified ON the face (§7.6 `exact.anchor`) — while it was the
# face's `gp_Pln` LOCATION a correct, unambiguous host scored 0.75 and re-picking
# re-froze the same miss, so the halt could never be cleared
# (`src-tauri/tests/step_import_gate.rs` PHASE 4, measured 0.75 → 1.00).
#
# The anchors below are the cap's own centre for that reason, not its plane origin.
#
# GEOMETRY. `plane:{kind:"XY"}` maps sketch u to world +Y and v to world −X
# (`worker/src/sketch/Sketch.h`), so the u∈[0,100] v∈[0,60] rectangle is a plate at
# world x∈[−60,0], y∈[0,100] and the circle at sketch (200,0) is a Ø40 cylinder on
# the world Z line through (0,200). The host sketch's own plane is the `custom`
# form `plane_from_point_normal` produces for a +Z normal — the NORMATIVE XY basis
# (xAxis=(0,1,0), yAxis=(−1,0,0)) at the cap's own origin — so a frame silently
# re-derived anywhere would show up as a different basis, not a coincidental match.
